WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.424
Multiple references to heap memory
complicate safety.

2
00:00:03.504 --> 00:00:06.677
A pointer may be owned by one component,
borrowed by another,

3
00:00:06.757 --> 00:00:09.059
cached in a structure, passed to a
callback,

4
00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:11.939
or used as an interior reference into a
larger object.

5
00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:15.814
Each reference needs a lifetime that does
not exceed the object it names.

6
00:00:15.894 --> 00:00:17.760
Shared mutation needs clear rules.

7
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.266
If two modules can modify the same heap
object,

8
00:00:20.346 --> 00:00:24.701
reviewers need to know which invariants
must be preserved and which operations can

9
00:00:24.781 --> 00:00:26.212
resize or release storage.

10
00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:30.461
Without those rules, one caller can
invalidate another caller's assumptions.

11
00:00:30.541 --> 00:00:32.905
Resizing or freeing can invalidate
aliases.

12
00:00:32.985 --> 00:00:36.469
A pointer that was safe before a resize
may become stale afterward.

13
00:00:36.549 --> 00:00:40.344
A pointer that was safe before cleanup may
become invalid after release.

14
00:00:40.424 --> 00:00:44.779
APIs that expose interior pointers should
document when those pointers stop being

15
00:00:44.859 --> 00:00:45.232
valid.

16
00:00:45.312 --> 00:00:48.174
Const-correctness can communicate intended
access.

17
00:00:48.254 --> 00:00:50.929
A const pointer does not solve ownership
by itself,

18
00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:54.369
but it can tell callers when data should
be treated as read-only.

19
00:00:54.449 --> 00:00:57.684
That makes accidental mutation easier to
spot during review.

20
00:00:57.764 --> 00:00:59.879
Safer APIs reduce ambiguous ownership.

21
00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:03.443
Prefer functions that clearly borrow,
clearly return ownership,

22
00:01:03.523 --> 00:01:06.074
or operate through a well-defined owner
object.

23
00:01:06.154 --> 00:01:10.322
Avoid designs where any caller can free
shared storage or where size metadata is

24
00:01:10.402 --> 00:01:12.580
separated from the pointer it describes.
